e person  walks along the street to the  box posts a letter walks away  the person with my child's level of  dyspraxia walks up a steep hill  watching the ground so they don't turn  an ankle on root judges the angle of the  post box mouth to the angle of the  letter insertion post a letter  then walks cautiously back down that  steep hill  and repeat that same level of extra  effort  every day in this country  as in any majority ethnically european  country especially one that has  colonial adventurism in its dna  in such a country if you are a child of  colour  the effort of your daily life is like  that of the dyspraxic child  the difficulty for that child of color  here encountered  is not one of inherited physical  disability but the disability of  projected  social prejudice and assumption so the  weight of that projected social  prejudice  mitigates daily against our confidence  and presses on us a relentless internal  conversation  questioning our gifts our intuition our  responses it's debilitating  and it demands huge reserves of  concentration self-reflection  and discernment to hold one's ground  i don't want to be here having this  conversation i have bok choy and  broccoli outside that needs planting  but for all the young me the young  hyundais the young wills the young  sineads  the young joanna's i want to share  through my work the richness of who we  are that we are all of equal value  and work worthy of equal interest simply  because we exist  and that's enough so to the point the  original  question should we applaud the  overcoming of historical accuracy in  favor of racially blind representation  and ignore race as a relevant  characteristic in all circumstances  or is color blindness a liberal mistake  a dangerous denial  of racial identity in a long history of  white supremacy  so i have to say depositing of this  question tonight speaks to whether the  event organizes  ignorance or more likely to their desire  to provoke  but indeed it speaks also to our  national ignorance as a country  of our history and the place of a range  of heritages and contributions within it  but kyende can speak with more authority  on that  i've had 30 years of my children in  education one of them is a teacher my  mother was also a history teacher  and i don't know about you but over  those 30 years it seemed to me that my  kids mainly learned about the tudors  and the nazis and then not even about  the huge trading and multicultural  life of the tudors nor the military  service and sacrifice of the colonial  armed forces in all the wars  or the murder in concentration camps  across  europe under the nazis of europe's  existing populations of color  without a proper assessment of our  history  no wonder we are confused about who we  are in this a historical fantasyland  there is always a conversation to be had  about the man-made barriers of class  and race and gender and ability  disability and sexuality and income and  caste  but that conversation is always also  about the integration  and contribution of that shared humanity  the reality of the lives lived takes  take nelson's column  the freeze at its base nelson is being  held by a black sailor because a fifth  of the british navy at that time  approximately was black so to that  question  let's overcome historical inaccuracy in  favor of racially  accurate representation and gender and  class and sexuality etc  so that's a lot of dramas we'd have to  ditch even really contemporary stuff  have another go with lots of historical  dramas war movies  the first british troops sent into ipr  in 1916  to road test the effects of mustard gas  on the human lung  for example with the british indian  regiments who died in their tens of  thousands for king and country queen  charlotte  wife to george iii not helen mirren i'm  afraid mixed race  daughter of alfonso the third of  portugal and his african mistress  who of course has no name it's in  contemporary documents of the time so  meghan markle late to that party i'm  afraid i've just filmed a netflix series  with  a mixed race queen charlotte in which i  play a director duchess  bridgeton coming soon so what we're  talking about here is reintegrating our  history  i want to turn now to kyende andrews for  the same question  um which is is race relevant to  historical stories and portrayals  uh yes so race is always relevant and  the color blindness is always a bad idea  and i give you lots of examples and  where it's a bad idea but generally  to ignore race and to ignore color is to  uh just give space to whiteness that's  what we're really talking about  because this is the idea that when we  talk about race you're talking about  people of color  but white people have race and in fact  white people's race has probably been  the most important because it is shaped  history culture etc etc so when you say  color blindness we're really saying  let's um ignore the critique of white  and in the in the tvs and in the art  production you can see this really  really really clearly  and it's more than just changing people  who are in  the cast it's actually about the stories  that we tell so the stories that we're  telling are  are the stories that we are comfortable  with as a society  they are the lady was saying it's the  it's the war movies  um i mean i can't i genuinely think  that's all we watched in school  was war movies and movies about the  royal family right it's these period  pieces  where we go back and we romanticize and  think about history in this way  one of the only things we really talk  about critically in in culture  and feel comfortable in in britain is  class so there's all these different  issues about class and isn't it great  we've overcome class inequality et  cetera et cetera so there's this  there's this comfort in this support  narrative  of the society that we're in there's a  more dangerous version of this as well  which is where actually we imagine that  those